WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.943
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.943 --> 00:00:02.222
Thursday the fifth.

00:00:02.222 --> 00:00:07.475
A plain one on the calendar, and the weather's the thing that changes tonight.

00:00:07.475 --> 00:00:09.967
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:17.038
When a stranger copies a public repo and sends a change, what's the one thing their test run doesn't get?

00:00:17.038 --> 00:00:18.385
Answer's at the end.

00:00:18.385 --> 00:00:19.598
Weather's turning.

00:00:19.598 --> 00:00:23.302
Light showers come and go this morning, nothing steady.

00:00:23.302 --> 00:00:30.103
The real rain moves in tonight and keeps going on and off through Friday, with some thunder mixed in.

00:00:30.103 --> 00:00:35.020
I couldn't pull temperatures or wind this morning, so that part's on you.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:35.963
Okay, the day.

00:00:35.963 --> 00:00:39.936
Calendar has an easy zone two run at seven, thirty minutes.

00:00:39.936 --> 00:00:41.822
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:41.822 --> 00:00:45.256
Nothing on there after that, so the evening's open.

00:00:45.256 --> 00:00:46.199
Alright, news.

00:00:46.199 --> 00:00:47.613
One from your corner.

00:00:47.613 --> 00:00:52.866
OpenAI put out a new version of its image model, ChatGPT Images two point six.

00:00:52.866 --> 00:00:58.860
It's faster, it takes reference images, and it can turn a still into a short looping GIF.

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:09.097
The part that matters for product work is a more detailed mode built to keep a product, a character, or a logo looking the same across a bunch of edits.

00:01:09.097 --> 00:01:11.858
That's been the weak spot of these tools.

00:01:11.858 --> 00:01:14.619
And one about photographers getting paid.

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:21.690
About twenty food photographers are suing Uber Eats, saying the app used their photos without permission.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:27.549
Uber argued it's not on them, because restaurants are the ones who upload the pictures.

00:01:27.549 --> 00:01:32.735
This week a federal judge in Florida said that doesn't fly, at least for now.

00:01:32.735 --> 00:01:39.335
Uber reviews and edits those images, and it earns a cut of every order, so the case moves forward.

00:01:39.335 --> 00:01:44.588
It's early, but if it holds, platforms can't just point at whoever hit upload.

00:01:44.588 --> 00:01:48.090
Further away, Israel's election ended in a deadlock.

00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:55.632
Netanyahu's side has fifty-three seats, his main rival's side has forty-eight, and you need sixty-one to govern.

00:01:55.632 --> 00:02:00.481
The president is meeting the parties now, and the last ones go in today.

00:02:00.481 --> 00:02:04.320
Whoever he picks gets about a month to build a coalition.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.495
If nobody can, Israel heads back to the polls early next year.

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:10.583
Okay, here's one for next week.

00:02:10.583 --> 00:02:18.193
Amant, the art space on Maujer Street in East Williamsburg, has turned its gallery into a working pottery studio.

00:02:18.193 --> 00:02:19.877
It's called The Mud Room.

00:02:19.877 --> 00:02:26.746
There are throwing wheels, a kiln, and a little stage in the middle, wrapped in big painted backdrops.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:29.507
The night to go is Friday the thirteenth.

00:02:29.507 --> 00:02:36.444
They're open till nine on Fridays, and there's a one-time performance at seven, made with a ceramicist.

00:02:36.444 --> 00:02:40.148
So you could wander the studio, then stay for the show.

00:02:40.148 --> 00:02:44.525
Since you're into making, it's a fun look at someone else's shop.

00:02:44.525 --> 00:02:49.307
The show runs through the end of the month if that Friday doesn't work.

00:02:49.307 --> 00:02:51.529
Today's challenge is a photo one.

00:02:51.529 --> 00:02:55.570
Tonight, once the streets are wet, make one reflection shot.

00:02:55.570 --> 00:03:02.776
Get your phone low, almost touching the ground, right at the edge of a puddle or a shiny patch of pavement.

00:03:02.776 --> 00:03:06.682
Then line up a streetlight, a sign, or a lit window in it.

00:03:06.682 --> 00:03:09.982
The lower you go, the bigger the reflection gets.

00:03:09.982 --> 00:03:11.935
One frame you like is enough.

00:03:11.935 --> 00:03:15.369
Okay, something new today, and it's a lighting one.

00:03:15.369 --> 00:03:18.400
Why color temperature numbers feel backwards.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:25.269
On a camera, a low number like twenty-eight hundred Kelvin means warm, orange light, like an old bulb.

00:03:25.269 --> 00:03:30.657
A high number like sixty-five hundred means cool, blue light, like a cloudy sky.

00:03:30.657 --> 00:03:33.149
But in everyday talk, warm means hot.

00:03:33.149 --> 00:03:36.381
So why is the hot-sounding color the low number?

00:03:36.381 --> 00:03:39.883
The scale comes from heating a chunk of black metal.

00:03:39.883 --> 00:03:41.769
As it gets hotter, it glows.

00:03:41.769 --> 00:03:49.042
First a dull red, then orange, then yellow, then white, and if you could push it far enough, a bluish white.

00:03:49.042 --> 00:03:52.275
Each color matches a real temperature in Kelvin.

00:03:52.275 --> 00:03:57.528
So the number is literally how hot that metal would be to give off that color.

00:03:57.528 --> 00:03:59.952
Blue light really is the hotter one.

00:03:59.952 --> 00:04:03.521
The art words, warm and cool, just run the other way.

00:04:03.521 --> 00:04:06.485
Here's the part that trips people up on set.

00:04:06.485 --> 00:04:11.131
The white balance setting on your camera isn't describing your light.

00:04:11.131 --> 00:04:12.478
It's the correction.

00:04:12.478 --> 00:04:21.839
If you set the camera to twenty-eight hundred, you're telling it the light is orange, so it pushes everything toward blue to cancel it out.

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:25.947
Set it too low under daylight, and the whole frame goes blue.

00:04:25.947 --> 00:04:28.507
That's also why mixed light is a pain.

00:04:28.507 --> 00:04:36.251
A window at around fifty-five hundred and a lamp at around twenty-eight hundred can't both be neutral in one frame.

00:04:36.251 --> 00:04:39.888
You pick one to match, and the other one shifts color.

00:04:39.888 --> 00:04:44.535
So: calendar says run at seven, Godinger till five, and a free night.

00:04:44.535 --> 00:04:46.218
Go grab that puddle shot.

00:04:46.218 --> 00:04:47.902
The answer: your secrets.

00:04:47.902 --> 00:04:49.990
Their run never sees your keys.

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:51.000
Stay dry, Ziad.
